  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nEarly afternoon RAP analysis and water vapor imagery reveal midlevel \nridging moving towards the central Mississippi valley, with midlevel \nlows closing off over southern Idaho, Florida, and Hudson Bay. A \nshortwave trough is swinging southeast of the latter feature into \nOntario. At the surface, broad low pressure is stretched out from \nKansas northeastward into IA, with resulting WAA into WI touching \noff light rain showers particularly over the southern portions of \nthe state. Still, some light rain showers have been able to make it \ninto the south-central UP. These could drop a couple hundredths of \nan inch of rain mainly over Menominee and Delta county through the \nrest of the afternoon, but significant impacts are not expected apart \nfrom persistent cloudiness across the UP. In spite of the cloud \ncover, temperatures across the area are climbing comfortably into \nthe mid 60s for most, with some spots even flirting with the upper \n60s. \n\nTonight, a cold front associated with the Ontario trough drops \nsoutheastward over Lake Superior and into the UP. This may bring \nsome spotty rain showers to the western and central UP, but they \nwill have to contend with dry midlevel air...and will note that most \nof the CAMs show these dying out soon  upon reaching our shores. \nInto the daytime hours, a surge of cooler air aloft will cause \nmarginally-supportive thermodynamic profiles for some lake-enhanced \nor lake-effect rainfall, with CAMs showing the orographic forcing of \nthe western and north-central UP being enough to force weak \nprecipitation. Thus, will maintain chance pops and another couple \nhundreds of QPF. This will also come with some gustier NE winds, \nwith gusts of 20+ MPH forecast especially along the Lake Superior \nshores. This in turn cranks wave heights up to 6 ft within 5 miles \nof the Lake Superior shoreline, which will drastically increase the \nrisk of hazardous rip currents and challenging swimming conditions \nfor those trying to squeeze in a late-September Sunday beach day at \nLake Superior beaches.\n\nIn the wake of the weekend disturbances, the western CONUS trough \nwill shift over the Dakotas by Sunday, weakening slightly. At the \nsame time, ridging to the north of the trough amplifies, causing \nsplit flow. This results in dominant surface high pressure \nestablishing over northern Ontario and Quebec through much of next \nweek, likely bringing an extended period of dry weather to the UP. \nThe latest NBM keeps us dry even through next weekend as ensembles \nflip-flop on whether ridging will persist over the region. Upstream \nridging aloft results in cooler continental flow aloft, and being on \nthe south/southwest periphery of a surface high results in cool \neasterly to northeasterly flow at the surface off of the Canadian \nShield. This brings cool conditions for the beginning of the week \nwith a gradual warming trend as the week goes on. For instance, the \nNBM even calls for up to 50% chances of sub-freezing temperatures \nfor Tuesday morning, though the LREF chances are significantly \nlower. By the end of the week, NBM highs are widespread 70+. The CPC \n6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks both show 50+% chances for the UP to be \nin above average temperatures for the end of September and a slight \nfavoring of below normal precipitation (30-40% chance).",
